From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hjviewer <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 22:06:05 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 071/162] hjviewer: phone targets, back, system bars and safe
 areas

U57: on a phone the most used controls keep their look and get larger
targets. The peer bar is 44 px high with the 32 px pills in it; its
search, refresh, select, sort and more icons are 44 x 44 targets around
their 26 px boxes, and the open search's close is a 36 x 44 target over
the field's right end. hjvPhoneBarShownActions (hjv_phone.dart, plain
Dart with a test) plans the bar's right side with these sizes and keeps
the room of the open search field; where the bar is short (a 360 dp
phone with four tabs) the refresh goes under "more" with the others.
The list of PCs refreshes when pulled down (RefreshIndicator.adaptive,
the same pull as the refresh button). A1's "have an access password?"
link is a 48 px target on a phone, menu rows are 48 px on a phone, the
connect button's 44 px circle sits in a 48 px target in the place it
had, and the session's keys are at least 40 x 40, 6 px apart. On a
desktop the session tab's close is a 24 px wide target as high as the
pill, its 16 px hover square unchanged.

U60: MainActivity sets android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="false", so
on Android 16 back reaches the home's WillPopScope and the dialogs'
back handling as on older versions instead of leaving the app; U63
replaces it with PopScope later. A test pins the attribute; it still
needs a check on an Android 16 device.

U61: the AppBar's system overlay style is hjvSystemUiOverlayStyle per
theme instead of Flutter's dark and light presets, which also paint the
navigation bar black with light icons: a transparent status bar, the
icons' brightness of the theme, the surface colour under the navigation
bar with the border as its divider, and no enforced contrast scrim.

U62: the phone home puts each tab's body in a SafeArea without top and
bottom, so in landscape the ID field, G3, the cards and the settings
stay clear of the notch, the camera hole and the rounded corners.
